WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.579 --> 00:00:05.099
When I first saw Star Wars as a kid, I was blown away by the size of the Death Star.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:07.350
I mean, it was an entire man-made moon!

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:10.470
Obi-Wan: That's no moon.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:12.760
It's a space station.

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:17.720
More recently, however, I learned how big the Death Star really is.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:24.560
This is a yoga ball, and if Earth was scaled down to the size of this, the Death Star would be as big as an

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:27.540
Airsoft BB. This is the destroyer of worlds?

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.080
mm. *eats bb*

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:32.400
We never get to see any Star Wars spaceships in any sort of physical

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:37.400
context, 'cause they're always next to like, planets, or in outer space, or next to arbitrary sci-fi cities,

00:00:37.409 --> 00:00:41.340
so that got me wondering what they would look like if I put them in places you're familiar with, so in today's video

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:44.159
that's exactly what I'm gonna do with the goal of helping you understand

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.959
just how big these spaceships from Star Wars really are. It's actually kind of crazy how many

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:53.879
iconic ship designs emerged from the Star Wars universe. Ah the Millennium Falcon

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:59.100
It's about as long as a Boeing 737 and stretches three times further than a city bus!

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:03.460
If this thing was parked on the highway, it would take up seven lanes of traffic! Talk about a traffic jam.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:05.580
Thanks Han. And the Imperial AT-AT,

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:10.140
which, honestly, I don't really have much to say, other than I just wanted to render it on our street. *chuckles*

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:14.820
Hey enough of them small fries, let's get to some big ships. The imperial star destroyer is

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:19.620
1600 meters long which coincidentally is the same length as a mile, so funny enough

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:22.580
it actually doubles as a pretty good unit of distance here in America.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:26.250
It's also interesting to see it next to the tallest building in the world, in Dubai (the Burj Khalifa)

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:30.500
It's like a giant, mile tall skyscraper, which actually gives me an idea for a future video

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:32.860
so, uhh, hit that notification bell for that.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:36.060
For our next ship though, we're gonna have to dip into the prequels a little bit.

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:40.160
*kazoo canteena theme plays*

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:43.080
The Trade Federation battle station.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:48.260
You remember it? That ship that looks like someone took a bite out of a donut and then spat it back out into the middle.

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.439
[Laughs]

00:01:50.439 --> 00:01:52.110
I mean come on, it does look like it though.

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:55.499
This battle station is over three kilometers in diameter making the

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.400
Imperial star destroyer basically the radius of it. Like the hands of a clock.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:05.100
And of course, Anakin saw that ship as a kid was like, "Meh. Someday, I'm gonna have a ship that is 19,000 meters long."

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:10.720
And he was right! A super star destroyer called "The Executor" ended up becoming Darth Vader's flagship vehicle

00:02:10.989 --> 00:02:17.219
that was indeed 19 kilometers long. It was twelve times longer than those wimpy little star destroyers,

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.820
what are those? Starships for ants?

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:26.060
I say it was longer because if you've seen Return of the Jedi, you'll know that it crashed into the Death Star like a freaking lawn dart.

00:02:26.069 --> 00:02:30.359
But all right. I know you've been waiting for it: the Death Stars. How big were they?

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:34.979
Well, the first one was 120 kilometers wide, which is legit pretty dang huge,

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.610
but as I've said, it's just like nothing in comparison to an actual planet.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:44.399
Oh, and the last jet he actually introduced that massive beast of a starship. That was Snoke's flagship vehicle.

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:51.269
This thing was 60 kilometers wide, so literally, Snoke's ship could be the radius of the Death Star.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:56.039
What is up with Star Wars and their obsession with making ships that could be radii of other ships?

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:59.519
And hey, at least it had the coolest death of any Star Wars ship ever.

00:02:59.519 --> 00:03:01.519
*hyperdrive engages*

00:03:01.519 --> 00:03:03.519
holy crap thats a big collision

00:03:03.519 --> 00:03:05.519
woah that looks like ice or something

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.800
And that brings us to the second Death Star: the one that never completed construction.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:17.840
According to Canon, It is 160 kilometers wide, which is 33% larger than the first station

00:03:17.849 --> 00:03:20.909
I always thought it was the same size of the first one, but no, apparently it's bigger.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:21.420
However,

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:22.860
remember that lawn dart scene?

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.819
The problem is that you should be able to clearly see the curvature of the Death Star. The lack of

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.000
curvature actually indicates that it's about 900 kilometers in diameter,

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:31.500
which

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:32.000
is

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.080
MASSIVE!

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:33.860
Its so big!

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:37.960
However, I need more examples than just one to really convince me that it is larger

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.440
so I'm gonna stick with the officially stated size.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.240
And the biggest on our list is the Starkiller base from the Force Awakens because it is

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:50.020
660 kilometers wide, making it the largest station in all of Star Wars

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:54.390
according-- according to Canon. I always thought of it as an actual planet

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:57.820
that was just simply repurposed into a star sucking weapon.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:00.960
Instead we're left with something that takes up 34 times less volume

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:02.489
than our own moon.

00:04:02.489 --> 00:04:06.419
Maybe we should actually be complementing the first-order on their ways of making efficient weapons.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:10.060
But, like, for real though, that thing destroyed like five planets and a star.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:12.060
And the thing I found most surprising from making this video is that

00:04:12.329 --> 00:04:14.720
All the smaller ships, like the big smaller ships,

00:04:14.739 --> 00:04:19.199
they seemed massive, and yet the ones that actually are massive seem kind of quaint

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:23.720
Anyway, this video gave me a whole new perspective on the universe and I hope you did for you, too.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:29.840
In fact, if you know anyone who could also use this new perspective, consider sending this video to them and thanks for watching.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:33.200
I hope my name pronunciations didn't trigger you too much

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:34.940
*wink*

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:50.740
*kazoo canteena music plays again*

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:54.700
this was a cool video

